1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,112
Welcome to all of you.., can everybody see and hear me?...

2
00:00:04,112 --> 00:00:05,200
Good...

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:09,860
I'm Sally, your guide for this tour of the Bicentennial Park...

4
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:12,493
I hope that you're all wearing your most comfortable shoes

5
00:00:12,493 --> 00:00:14,600
and that you can keep up the pace.

6
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:19,240
So let's get under way on our tour around this wonderful park.

7
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:23,400
I'll start today with some general background information.

8
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:28,200
There used to be a lot of factories in this area until the 1960s.

9
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:34,090
Creating the park required the demolition of lots of derelict buildings on the site,

10
00:00:34,090 --> 00:00:39,559
so most of the exciting park space all around you was originally warehouses

11
00:00:39,559 --> 00:00:40,400
and storehouses.

12
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:46,770
The idea of building a public park here was first discussed when a property

13
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:49,500
developer proposed a high-rise housing development,

14
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:52,230
but the local community wasn't happy.

15
00:00:52,230 --> 00:00:57,865
If the land was to be cleaned up, they wanted to use the site

16
00:00:57,865 --> 00:00:58,670
for recreation.

17
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:02,695
Residents wanted open space for outdoor activities, rather than housing

18
00:01:02,695 --> 00:01:05,110
or even an indoor sports complex.

19
00:01:05,110 --> 00:01:09,190
Now to the Bicentennial Park itself.

20
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:15,149
It has two areas, a nature reserve and a formal park with man-made features

21
00:01:15,149 --> 00:01:16,000
and gardens.

22
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,286
The tall blue-and-white building in front of us is called The Tower

23
00:01:20,286 --> 00:01:23,500
and is the centre point for the formal gardens.

24
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:25,500
It stands twelve metres high,

25
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:31,100
so follow me up the stairs to where we can take advantage of the

26
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:31,900
fantastic views.

27
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:36,057
Well, here we are at the top of The Tower,

28
00:01:36,057 --> 00:01:40,630
and we're going to look at the view from each direction.

29
00:01:40,630 --> 00:01:45,959
Out to the east, the large buildings about a kilometre away are on the

30
00:01:45,959 --> 00:01:46,720
Olympic site.

31
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:51,705
There's an indoor arena for gymnastics, a stadium for track and field

32
00:01:51,705 --> 00:01:56,690
and a swimming pool for races and synchronised swimming and also diving.

33
00:01:56,690 --> 00:02:01,860
If you look carefully down there, you can see the train lines.

34
00:02:01,860 --> 00:02:06,500
The Olympic site has its own station to encourage the use of public transport.

35
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:08,244
There is also a car park,

36
00:02:08,244 --> 00:02:10,860
but it only holds a limited number of cars.

37
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:15,000
The formal park has some specially-created water features.

38
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,133
If you look out here to the south, you can see a circular ornamental

39
00:02:20,133 --> 00:02:20,500
pond.

40
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:25,613
And around to the west, you can relax and sit on a bench to

41
00:02:25,613 --> 00:02:28,170
smell the flowers in the rose garden,

42
00:02:28,170 --> 00:02:32,356
and finally up to the north, if you look in front of you now,

43
00:02:32,356 --> 00:02:36,541
there's a lake with a small island in the centre, you can hire rowing

44
00:02:36,541 --> 00:02:38,036
boats at the boat shed,

45
00:02:38,036 --> 00:02:39,830
which you can't see from here,

46
00:02:39,830 --> 00:02:43,323
but if you look through the trees, you can see the café,

47
00:02:43,323 --> 00:02:45,360
which has lovely views across the water.

48
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:48,460
OK, let's climb down now.

49
00:02:48,460 --> 00:02:52,369
We will go now and have a look at the nature reserve section, of

50
00:02:52,369 --> 00:02:52,927
the park,

51
00:02:52,927 --> 00:02:55,440
which has opened up natural wetland to the public.

52
00:02:55,440 --> 00:03:00,966
The Mangroves have been made more accessible to visitors by the boardwalk built during

53
00:03:00,966 --> 00:03:02,150
the park's upgrade.

54
00:03:02,150 --> 00:03:06,231
You'd think that people would come here to look at the unusual plant life

55
00:03:06,231 --> 00:03:07,105
of the area,

56
00:03:07,105 --> 00:03:11,186
but in fact it's more often used for cycling and is very popular with

57
00:03:11,186 --> 00:03:12,060
the local clubs.

58
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:16,488
This is the far end of the park and over there you can see

59
00:03:16,488 --> 00:03:20,600
the Frog Pond, a natural feature here long before the park was designed.

60
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:25,883
Just next to it we have our outdoor classroom, a favourite spot for school

61
00:03:25,883 --> 00:03:26,260
parties.

62
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:31,180
The area is now most often used by primary schools for biology lessons.

63
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:35,720
And finally let's pass by the Waterbird Refuge.

64
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:39,059
This area is in a sheltered part of the estuary,

65
00:03:39,059 --> 00:03:43,733
that's why the park's viewing shelter is a favourite spot for bird watchers who

66
00:03:43,733 --> 00:03:46,070
can use it to spy through binoculars.

67
00:03:46,070 --> 00:03:48,699
You can watch a variety of water birds,

68
00:03:48,699 --> 00:03:53,300
but most visitors expect to see black swans when they come to the shelter.

69
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:56,000
You might spot one yourself right now!

70
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,600
Well, here we are back at our starting point, the Visitor Centre.
